---
import Landing from "../../layouts/Landing.astro";
---

<Landing
  title="AI pixel art for Godot 4 · PAGAN"
  description="Let your AI agent make pixel art for your Godot 4 game: SpriteFrames and TileSet resources, 8-16 consistent headings from 3D models, verified in headless Godot. Free and open source."
  heading="AI pixel art for Godot 4, as resources Godot loads"
  points={[
    "<b>SpriteFrames</b> <code>.tres</code> for every sprite and model: one animation per heading (<code>walk_se</code>, <code>attack_n</code>…).",
    "<b>TileSet</b> <code>.tres</code> for tilesets, including animated tiles; atlas coordinates in <code>manifest.json</code>.",
    "Pivots as <code>godot_offset</code>, normal maps for Godot's 2D lights, team colours as variant sheets or swap tables.",
    "Every resource format is copied from what Godot 4.7's ResourceSaver writes and verified by loading it in headless Godot.",
    "Free, MIT, offline. Your agent writes the art; you review it like code.",
  ]}
  code={`# pagan.toml, inside your Godot project
output_dir = "../assets/generated"
export = ["png", "godot"]

# in GDScript
$Zombie.sprite_frames = load("res://assets/generated/sprites/zombie.tres")
$Zombie.offset = Vector2(0, -17)   # manifest: godot_offset
$Zombie.play("walk_" + heading)`}
>
  <p>
    PAGAN is a text language for game art. Your agent (Claude with the PAGAN skill, or any agent over
    MCP) writes the sprites, tiles and 3D models; <code>pagan build</code> turns them into PNG sheets and
    Godot resources that drop straight into your project.
  </p>
</Landing>
